<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/advanced/modernism-and-postmodernism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Modernism and postmodernism are <strong>literature's responses to the 20th century's double
    rupture</strong>: modernism (1910–45) shattered traditional form to capture a shattered world;
    postmodernism (1945–2000) treated all form — including modernism's — as play. Reading their
    techniques is reading how writers answered the century's crises.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the break, the techniques, the play, and the line between them" widget="equationStep" config={modernCfg} />

  <h2>The landmark texts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ulysses (1922):</strong> one day, eighteen episodes, every prose style in English history — the book that made modernism's case and scandalised two continents (banned in the US until 1933).</li>
    <li><strong>The Waste Land (1922):</strong> published the same year — a poem of fragments, five languages, footnotes. Eliot's "mythic method" (using the Grail legend to organise the chaos) became modernism's organising move.</li>
    <li><strong>Beloved (1987) and the postmodern turn toward history:</strong> Morrison blends ghost story with slavery's archive — postmodern technique in service of recovering suppressed memory. The best postmodernism is rarely just play.</li>
    <li><strong>Post-postmodernism ("new sincerity"):</strong> David Foster Wallace warned in 1993 that irony was exhausting itself; the last two decades of literature answer him with renewed sincerity inside formal sophistication.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Joyce (1922); Eliot (1922); McHale, Postmodernist Fiction (1987); Wallace, "E Unibus Pluram" (1993); Morrison (1987).</p>
</TopicPage>
